## Problem
Async operations in useEffect can cause state updates after component unmount.

## Solution
```typescript
useEffect(() => {
  let cancelled = false;
  async function fetchData() {
    const result = await api.getData();
    if (!cancelled) setState(result);
  }
  fetchData();
  return () => { cancelled = true; };
}, []);
```

## Key Insight
This pattern was discovered across 3 different projects where the same memory leak issue appeared.

## Problem
GraphQL resolvers with Prisma cause N+1 queries when resolving relations.

## Solution
Use DataLoader to batch database queries:
```typescript
const userLoader = new DataLoader(async (ids) => {
  const users = await prisma.user.findMany({ where: { id: { in: ids } } });
  return ids.map(id => users.find(u => u.id === id));
});
```

## Impact
Reduced API response time from 2.3s to 180ms on the users endpoint.

## Problem
Unhandled errors in Electron IPC handlers crash the main process silently.

## Solution
Create a wrapper function for all IPC handlers:
```typescript
function safeHandle(channel: string, handler: Function) {
  ipcMain.handle(channel, async (...args) => {
    try {
      return { success: true, data: await handler(...args) };
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(`[IPC] ${channel} error:`, error);
      return { success: false, error: error.message };
    }
  });
}
```

## Key Insight
This pattern prevents app crashes and provides consistent error reporting to the renderer process.

## Pattern
```typescript
export const useStore = create<StoreType>((set, get) => ({
  items: [],
  loading: false,
  error: null,
  fetch: async () => {
    if (!window.electronAPI) return;
    set({ loading: true, error: null });
    try {
      const items = await window.electronAPI.service.list();
      set({ items, loading: false });
    } catch (err) {
      set({ error: err.message, loading: false });
    }
  },
}));
```

## Key Points
- Always check `window.electronAPI` exists
- Set loading state before async call
- Clear error state on new fetch
- Handle errors gracefully

## Applied In
Used consistently across 8 stores in ClaudeDeck project.

## Why Token Bucket?
Fixed window counters allow burst traffic at window boundaries. Token bucket provides smooth rate limiting.

## Implementation
```typescript
class TokenBucket {
  private tokens: number;
  private lastRefill: number;
  constructor(private capacity: number, private refillRate: number) {
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.lastRefill = Date.now();
  }
  consume(): boolean {
    this.refill();
    if (this.tokens > 0) { this.tokens--; return true; }
    return false;
  }
  private refill() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.lastRefill) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + elapsed * this.refillRate);
    this.lastRefill = now;
  }
}
```

## Setup
1. Configure Tailwind: `darkMode: 'class'`
2. Add theme toggle component
3. Detect system preference on load
4. Persist user choice in localStorage

## Pattern
```typescript
function useTheme() {
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState(() => {
    const saved = localStorage.getItem('theme');
    if (saved) return saved;
    return window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches ? 'dark' : 'light';
  });
  useEffect(() => {
    document.documentElement.classList.toggle('dark', theme === 'dark');
    localStorage.setItem('theme', theme);
  }, [theme]);
  return { theme, setTheme };
}
```

## Gotcha
Always use `dark:` prefix consistently. Missing dark variants cause jarring visual switches.

## Pattern
```typescript
class ReconnectingWebSocket {
  private retryCount = 0;
  private maxRetries = 10;
  private baseDelay = 1000;

  connect() {
    this.ws = new WebSocket(this.url);
    this.ws.onclose = () => this.scheduleReconnect();
  }

  private scheduleReconnect() {
    if (this.retryCount >= this.maxRetries) return;
    const delay = Math.min(
      this.baseDelay * Math.pow(2, this.retryCount) + Math.random() * 1000,
      30000
    );
    setTimeout(() => { this.retryCount++; this.connect(); }, delay);
  }
}
```

## Why Jitter?
Without jitter, all disconnected clients reconnect simultaneously, overwhelming the server.
